See it in 30 seconds <p>Change an entity, and Migrax writes the SQL and the script that undoes it.</p> Add a columnRename a columnNew tableRemove a column Customer.java<pre><code>   @Column(nullable = false, length = 100)
   String name;
+
+  @Column(length = 30)
+  String phone;
 }
</code></pre> 0002_add_customers_phone.sql<pre><code>ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN phone varchar(30);
</code></pre> rollback/0002_add_customers_phone.sql<pre><code>-- Note: deletes data written to customers.phone since the migration ran.
ALTER TABLE customers DROP COLUMN phone;
</code></pre> <p>The new column, and a rollback script that says which data undoing it would delete.</p> Customer.java<pre><code>-  @Column(nullable = false, length = 100)
-  String name;
+  @Column(name = "full_name", nullable = false, length = 100)
+  String fullName;
</code></pre> 0003_rename_customers_name.sql<pre><code>ALTER TABLE customers RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name;
</code></pre> rollback/0003_rename_customers_name.sql<pre><code>ALTER TABLE customers RENAME COLUMN full_name TO name;
</code></pre> <p>A dropped column next to a new one looks like a rename. Migrax asks, and renames it so the data stays; in CI it stops and tells you to pass <code>--rename</code>.</p> PurchaseOrder.java<pre><code>@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class PurchaseOrder {
  @Id
  @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  Long id;

  @ManyToOne(optional = false)
  Customer customer;

  @Column(nullable = false, precision = 12, scale = 2)
  BigDecimal total;
}
</code></pre> 0004_create_orders.sql<pre><code>CREATE TABLE orders (customer_id bigint NOT NULL, id bigint GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY NOT NULL, total numeric(12,2) NOT NULL, CONSTRAINT orders_pkey PRIMARY KEY (id));
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT fk_orders_customer_id_54d4cc52 FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers (id);
</code></pre> rollback/0004_create_orders.sql<pre><code>-- Note: deletes data written to orders since the migration ran.
ALTER TABLE orders DROP CONSTRAINT fk_orders_customer_id_54d4cc52;
DROP TABLE orders;
</code></pre> <p>Types and keys come from Hibernate's own mapping, so they match what Hibernate expects; unnamed constraints get readable names.</p> Customer.java<pre><code>   @Column(name = "full_name", nullable = false, length = 100)
   String fullName;
-
-  @Column(length = 30)
-  String phone;
 }
</code></pre> <pre><code>$ migrax generate
1 change(s) for postgresql:
  1. drop column customers.phone  [DESTRUCTIVE]
     ALTER TABLE customers DROP COLUMN phone;
error[MXE108]: 1 destructive change(s) detected (marked [DESTRUCTIVE] above).
</code></pre> rollback/0005_remove_customers_phone.sql<pre><code>-- WARNING: restores the structure of customers.phone but not its data.
ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN phone varchar(30);
</code></pre> <p>Nothing is dropped until you say <code>--allow-destructive</code>, and the rollback script warns that it can't bring the data back.</p> Why teams use Migrax <p>Everything a migration needs, checked before it reaches production.</p> <ul> <li> <p> Migrations written for you</p> <p>Change an entity, run <code>migrax generate</code>. Tables, columns, keys, indexes, join tables, element collections, sequences and inheritance come out exactly as your Hibernate, EclipseLink or OpenJPA maps them.</p> </li> <li> <p> Checked before production</p> <p>Every migration is linted for statements that lock big tables, fail on existing rows or break running instances. <code>migrax verify</code> checks the result against your mapping, and Hibernate validates it itself.</p> </li> <li> <p> Rollback included</p> <p>Each generated migration comes with a rollback script, and <code>verify</code> tests every one of them: roll back, apply again, same schema.</p> </li> <li> <p> Asks before it loses data</p> <p>A dropped column that looks renamed? Migrax asks, and renames it so the data stays. Drops only happen when you say <code>--allow-destructive</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p> Catches manual changes</p> <p><code>migrax drift</code> compares the live database with what your migrations produce and lists every difference, including columns someone made narrower.</p> </li> <li> <p> Zero setup</p> <p>Run it in your service folder. It compiles the project, finds the dependencies and reads the database settings from your application configuration.</p> </li> </ul> Works with your stack <p>Detected automatically. No plugin configuration needed to get started.</p> FrameworksSpring BootQuarkusMicronaut 4 and 5Helidon 4Jakarta EEPlain Hibernate Any languagePythonNode.jsGo.NETPHPRubyhand-written SQL migrations, how JPA providersHibernate 4.0 to 7.4EclipseLink 2.7 to 4.0OpenJPA 3.2 and 4.1Spring Data JPA and Micronaut Data JPA, through Hibernateeach read through its own mapping No JPASpring D