settings.gradle.kts<pre><code>pluginManagement {
    repositories {
        mavenCentral()
        gradlePluginPortal()
    }
}
</code></pre> build.gradle.kts<pre><code>plugins {
    java
    id("org.migrax") version "0.17.1"
}

repositories {
    mavenCentral()          // the plugin loads the Migrax library from here
}

migrax {
    packageName.set("com.example.shop")   // optional; defaults to the project group
}
</code></pre> <p>The plugin also adds <code>.migrax/snapshot.json</code> to the application's resources as <code>META-INF/migrax/snapshot.json</code>, so the application can check for drift at startup.</p>

pom.xml<pre><code>&lt;build&gt;
  &lt;plugins&gt;
    &lt;plugin&gt;
      &lt;groupId&gt;org.migrax&lt;/groupId&gt;
      &lt;artifactId&gt;migrax&lt;/artifactId&gt;
      &lt;version&gt;0.17.1&lt;/version&gt;
    &lt;/plugin&gt;
  &lt;/plugins&gt;
&lt;/build&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Maven downloads it from Maven Central. Goals compile the project and use its runtime classpath, so no other configuration is needed.</p>

Framework <code>datasource</code> names Example Spring Boot a <code>DataSource</code> bean <code>billingDataSource</code> Quarkus a named data source (<code>quarkus.datasource.&lt;name&gt;.*</code>) <code>billing</code> Micronaut a data source (<code>datasources.&lt;name&gt;.*</code>) <code>billing</code> Helidon a data source (<code>javax.sql.DataSource.&lt;name&gt;.*</code>) <code>billing</code> Jakarta EE a JNDI name <code>java:global/jdbc/billing</code> Spring BootQuarkusMicronautHelidonJakarta EE <p>An application with two databases defines both <code>DataSource</code> beans; the default database and JPA use the one marked <code>@Primary</code>:</p> <pre><code>@Bean
@Primary
@ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.orders")
DataSource dataSource() {
    return DataSourceBuilder.create().build();
}

@Bean
@ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.billing")
DataSource billingDataSource() {
    return DataSourceBuilder.create().build();
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>migrax.databases.billing.datasource=billingDataSource
</code></pre> <p>Every <code>EntityManagerFactory</code> waits until all databases are migrated. <code>JavaMigration</code> beans belong to the default database; a further database finds its Java migrations in its <code>java-package</code>.</p> <pre><code>quarkus.datasource.billing.db-kind=postgresql
quarkus.datasource.billing.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/billing
migrax.databases.billing.locations=db/billing
</code></pre> <p>The block's name is also the data source's name unless <code>datasource</code> says otherwise. The migrations are listed when the application is built, so native executables migrate every database too.</p> <pre><code>datasources:
  billing:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/billing
jpa:
  billing:
    entity-scan:
      packages: com.example.billing
migrax:
  databases:
    billing:
      locations: db/billing
</code></pre> <p>Micronaut starts Hibernate for each data source, so give each one its own <code>jpa.&lt;name&gt;</code> entities. Migrax creates every configured data source at startup, so a database is migrated even when nothing uses it yet.</p> <pre><code>javax.sql.DataSource.billing.dataSourceClassName=org.postgresql.ds.PGSimpleDataSource
javax.sql.DataSource.billing.dataSource.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/billing
migrax.databases.billing.locations=db/billing
</code></pre> migrax.properties<pre><code>migrax.databases.billing.datasource=java:global/jdbc/billing
</code></pre> <p>Use a global JNDI name: a data source defined in the server, or one the application defines under <code>java:global/</code>. Some servers, such as Payara, can't look up <code>java:app/</code> names yet while the application starts.</p>
